# Normalize line endings so genuine corruption stands out instead of drowning in
# CRLF-vs-LF noise (Windows host + Linux CI + sandbox clones all agree on LF).
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary

# ── CONTENT-PACK MEMBERS ([ADR 007]) — and `manifest.sig` is the dangerous one ─
# `text=auto` classifies a file as binary by looking for a NUL byte in its first
# 8000. An Ed25519 signature is 64 bytes of uniform noise: the committed one
# happens to contain no NUL, so git calls it TEXT and `eol=lf` becomes live on it.
# Today that is harmless because it also contains no 0x0D and no 0x0A — but the
# signature changes every time the pack does, and roughly one in four carries a
# CR or an LF. On the run where it does, checkout would rewrite the signature
# bytes and every client would reject a pack that was signed correctly, with the
# repository showing no diff. Marked explicitly rather than left to a heuristic
# that is right by luck. tooling/ci/assert-pack-inert.mjs asserts these stay here.
*.sig binary
*.webp binary
*.mp3 binary


# ── ADDED 2026-09-08, S1 phase 1 ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above already gives every text file LF, by a HEURISTIC.
# These six lines say the same thing for the four extensions an agent writes
# most, EXPLICITLY, so the rule survives a future narrowing of the wildcard and
# so the same six lines read identically in all three repositories. Private had
# no rule at all for them until today and its working tree disagreed with its
# index on every .md and .json in it.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
# .ps1 and .bat go the OTHER way, and this one is a real change rather than a
# restatement: `text=auto eol=lf` was handing tooling/windows-setup.ps1 LF
# endings. It is read by a Windows shell, is never copied to the Linux box, and
# CRLF is its native form. The index keeps LF either way, so nothing in history
# moves; only the checkout changes.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
